    title: 'Neuroscience of Motivation and Emotion',
    text: `Motivation can be divided into intrinsic motivation — engaging in a behavior for its own sake, for the inherent satisfaction or interest — and extrinsic motivation — engaging in a behavior for external rewards or to avoid punishment. Self-determination theory (Deci and Ryan) proposes that intrinsic motivation is supported by three basic psychological needs: autonomy (sense of choice and ownership), competence (sense of mastery), and relatedness (sense of connection). When external rewards undermine perceived autonomy, they can reduce intrinsic motivation — a phenomenon termed the overjustification effect.

Drive reduction theory (Hull) proposes that physiological needs (hunger, thirst, temperature regulation) create internal drives that motivate behavior aimed at restoring homeostasis. Incentive theory proposes that external stimuli (incentives) pull organisms toward goal-directed behavior; the incentive value of a stimulus is influenced by past experience and current deprivation state. Maslow's hierarchy of needs proposes that lower-order needs (physiological, safety) must be satisfied before higher-order needs (belonging, esteem, self-actualization) are pursued.

Emotion involves both physiological arousal and cognitive appraisal. The James-Lange theory holds that emotional experience is the perception of physiological change ("we are afraid because we tremble"). The Cannon-Bard theory holds that physiological arousal and emotional experience occur simultaneously and independently, both triggered by the thalamus. Schachter-Singer two-factor theory holds that emotion = physiological arousal + cognitive label; the same arousal can produce different emotions depending on the situational appraisal.

The limbic system — including the amygdala, hippocampus, hypothalamus, and cingulate cortex — is central to emotional processing. The amygdala processes fear and emotional salience; stimulation produces fear responses; damage causes Klüver-Bucy syndrome (placidity, hypersexuality, hyperorality, inability to recognize emotional significance of objects).`,
