    title: 'Group Behavior and Social Influence',
    text: `Social psychology investigates how individuals' thoughts, feelings, and behaviors are influenced by the actual, imagined, or implied presence of others. Several classic phenomena illuminate the power of social influence.

Conformity refers to changing one's behavior or beliefs to match a group. Asch's conformity experiments demonstrated that participants would give obviously incorrect answers when confederates unanimously gave the wrong answer — even when the task (judging line lengths) was unambiguous. Conformity was driven by normative influence (desire to be accepted) and informational influence (using the group as a source of information when the situation is ambiguous).

Obedience refers to compliance with direct orders from an authority figure. Milgram's obedience studies found that 65% of participants administered what they believed were painful, potentially lethal electric shocks to a confederate when instructed by an experimenter in a white coat. Factors increasing obedience included: physical proximity to the authority, proximity of the victim being low (buffering effect), and escalating commitment.

Bystander effect: the presence of other people reduces the likelihood that any individual will help in an emergency. Latané and Darley identified two key mechanisms: (1) diffusion of responsibility — each bystander assumes someone else will help; (2) pluralistic ignorance — each bystander looks to others for cues and, seeing no one act, interprets the situation as non-emergent.

Social facilitation refers to improved performance on simple or well-learned tasks in the presence of others, and impaired performance on complex or novel tasks. Zajonc proposed the dominant response hypothesis: presence of others increases arousal → strengthens the dominant (most practiced) response → helps if dominant response is correct (simple task), hurts if dominant response is incorrect (complex task).`,
