    title: 'Culture, Health, and Demographic Patterns',
    text: `Culture encompasses the shared values, norms, beliefs, symbols, and practices of a group. Cultural competence in medicine requires awareness of how cultural factors influence health beliefs, illness experience, healthcare seeking, and adherence. Explanatory models (Kleinman) refer to patients' own cultural frameworks for understanding their illness — what caused it, why it started when it did, what it does, how severe it is, and what treatment it requires. When provider and patient explanatory models diverge, miscommunication and poor adherence can result.

Health disparities are differences in health outcomes between groups that are systematic, avoidable, and often the result of social and economic disadvantage. Social determinants of health — including income, education, housing, neighborhood safety, and social support — account for approximately 30-55% of health outcomes. The built environment (the physical structures and infrastructure of neighborhoods) influences health: walkability promotes physical activity; food deserts limit access to nutritious food; environmental exposures (pollution, industrial sites) cluster in low-income and minority neighborhoods.

Demographic patterns in the United States show significant variation in health outcomes by race, ethnicity, SES, and geography. Black Americans have higher rates of cardiovascular disease, hypertension, diabetes, and maternal mortality than white Americans — even after controlling for SES in many analyses. Rural communities face challenges including healthcare provider shortages, limited specialty care access, and higher rates of chronic disease and opioid use disorder. The Hispanic paradox describes the phenomenon where Hispanic Americans — despite lower average SES — have better health outcomes on several measures (especially cardiovascular mortality) compared to non-Hispanic whites; proposed explanations include cultural protective factors (familismo, social cohesion), selective migration effects, and possibly data limitations.

Medically underserved areas (MUAs) and health professional shortage areas (HPSAs) are federal designations for regions lacking adequate primary care access, often coinciding with low-income, rural, or urban minority communities.`,
